  "body": "Requested by the user (2026-11-11), who uses Saunders Machine Works \"Mod Vise\"-style modular vises on a fixture plate.\n\n**What:** a **plate-mounted modular vise** style in the built-in vise family (#788/PR #802; the self-centering style in PR #811 shows how a style branches). It's a low-profile vise body that bolts to a fixture plate's grid, with interchangeable jaws (hard and soft). Generic, original geometry from a few dimensions; no vendor model.\n- **Parameters:** body length, width and height; jaw width and height; max opening; a jaw body depth (as #802); and the mounting footprint (which grid holes or dowels it uses).\n- **Placement:** onto a fixture plate's grid (sibling issue), as one undoable command. The jaw opening is fitted to the stock as in #802. Several mod vises on one plate are common, so per-vise drafts are needed (#802 has them).\n- Collision checks use the existing fixture laws. Saved and loaded; MCP.\n\nEffort: high.\n\n**Done means:** a mod vise can be placed on the plate grid, its opening fitted to the stock, and it's judged by the fixture laws. Pinned. **Checked against the user's real mod vises.**\n\nDepends on #819 (fixture plates) for grid placement. Part of #339.\n",
